## 7.3 Prometheus Failure & Recovery

### Objective

Verify that the monitoring infrastructure itself can recover from a component failure.

The Prometheus pod was intentionally deleted:

```bash
sudo kubectl delete pod -n monitoring -l app=prometheus
```

### Recovery

Kubernetes immediately created a replacement Prometheus pod.

The replacement pod reached:

```text
1/1 Running
```

The new pod successfully initialized Prometheus.

Prometheus logs confirmed:

```text
Starting Prometheus Server
TSDB started
Completed loading of configuration file
Server is ready to receive web requests.
```

The replacement pod was:

```text
prometheus-545899ff95-5gv7g   1/1   Running
```

### Result

**PASS — Prometheus automatically recovered through Kubernetes deployment management.**

---

## 7.4 Database Integrity After Failure

### Objective

Verify that the worker failure and PostgreSQL pod recovery did not corrupt or alter the Data Warehouse.

The primary fact table was checked after recovery:

```sql
SELECT COUNT(*) AS total_fact_stock_prices
FROM warehouse.fact_stock_prices;
```

### Result

```text
total_fact_stock_prices
-----------------------
619017
```

Expected baseline:

```text
619017
```

Actual:

```text
619017
```

### Result

**PASS — Data integrity was preserved.**

The Data Warehouse retained all **619,017 fact records** after the failure-testing process.

---

## 7.5 PostgreSQL Monitoring Validation

### Objective

Verify that the PostgreSQL exporter correctly reports database availability.

The PostgreSQL exporter was queried directly:

```bash
sudo kubectl exec -n datawarehouse deploy/postgres-exporter -- \
wget -qO- http://localhost:9187/metrics | grep '^pg_up'
```

Result:

```text
pg_up 1
```

The Prometheus metric:

```promql
pg_up{job="postgres"}
```

therefore indicates that PostgreSQL is healthy.

### Result

**PASS — PostgreSQL health monitoring is functioning correctly.**

